<section>
    <h2>Two options… neither is great</h2>
    <table class="compare">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th></th>
                <th>Real HTTP calls</th>
                <th>Handwritten mocks</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Realism</td>
                <td class="good">Real responses</td>
                <td class="bad">What we imagine the API returns</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Speed</td>
                <td class="bad">Slow (network)</td>
                <td class="good">Instant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Stability in CI</td>
                <td class="bad">Depends on the provider (quotas, outages)</td>
                <td class="good">Stable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Cost</td>
                <td class="bad">Paying APIs bill every call (OpenAI, payments…)</td>
                <td class="good">Free</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Secrets</td>
                <td class="bad">API keys in CI</td>
                <td class="good">None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Maintenance</td>
                <td class="meh">None… as long as the API answers</td>
                <td class="bad">Tedious, and it drifts</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Let's do a quick comparison of the two ways we usually test this.</p>
        <p>Realism: real calls give us real responses. With mocks, we end up testing our imagination rather than the API.</p>
        <p>Speed: real calls go over the network, so they're slow. Mocks are instant.</p>
        <p>Stability: real calls depend on the provider, its quotas and its outages, so they're flaky. Mocks are stable.</p>
        <p>Cost: paying APIs bill every call, so every test run has a price. Mocks are free.</p>
        <p>Secrets: real calls need API keys in CI. Mocks need none.</p>
        <p>Maintenance: real calls need nothing, as long as the API answers. Mocks are tedious to write, and they drift: the day the API changes, the test stays green and production breaks.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
